menu "Siri Bridge Configuration"

    config WIFI_SSID
        string "Wi-Fi SSID"
        default ""
        help
            SSID for the ESP32 to join. Values land in sdkconfig, which is
            gitignored — set via `idf.py menuconfig`, never commit secrets to
            sdkconfig.defaults.

    config WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "Wi-Fi password"
        default ""
        help
            WPA2 password. Leave empty for open networks.

    config MQTT_BROKER_URI
        string "MQTT broker URI"
        default "mqtt://homeassistant.local:1883"
        help
            URI of the Home Assistant MQTT broker. Use mqtts:// for TLS.

    config MQTT_USERNAME
        string "MQTT username"
        default ""

    config MQTT_PASSWORD
        string "MQTT password"
        default ""

    config DEBUG_TOUCH_FRAMES
        bool "Log raw touch frames to UART (diagnostic)"
        default n
        help
            When enabled, every touch notification (handle 0x003D) is logged
            to UART as hex with parsed x/y/pressure/finger fields. Intended
            for protocol diagnosis — for example, understanding how the
            circular touchpad encodes coordinates near its physical edges,
            where small swipes are misclassified.

            Adds significant log spam during touchpad use (~50 lines/sec).
            Disable for normal operation.

    config DEBUG_WAKE_PROBE
        bool "Wake-press loss probe (diagnostic)"
        default n
        help
            When enabled, the BLE connect path is instrumented with millisecond
            timestamps relative to BLE_GAP_EVENT_CONNECT (logged as `t=Xms`),
            and apply_remote_setup additionally subscribes to every
            unexplored notify-capable HID Report CCCD on the gen-3 remote
            (handles 0x0042 / 0x0046 / 0x004A / 0x0051 / 0x0054 / 0x0056 /
            0x0058). Combined with the existing notify-RX hex dump, this
            captures whether any unexplored handle carries the wake-button
            identity that's lost on cold-boot connects.

            Diagnostic only — leave disabled for normal operation.

    config BATTERY_LOW_THRESHOLD_PCT
        int "Battery low threshold (percent)"
        default 20
        range 0 100
        help
            When battery level is at or below this percentage, the published
            battery JSON includes "low": true. Set to 0 to always report
            "low": false (effectively disabling the alert field). HA can also
            do this kind of thresholding template-side; this knob just lets
            firmware-only setups have a sane low-battery flag.

    config IDLE_DISCONNECT_MS
        int "Idle BLE disconnect threshold (ms)"
        default 0
        range 0 3600000
        help
            After this many milliseconds with no notifications from the
            remote, the bridge proactively terminates the BLE connection.
            **Default is 0 (always-connected mode), which is the
            recommended setting** — see below.

            Wake-press loss caveat: Apple's BLE accessory firmware drops
            the press that wakes the remote if the bridge has been
            disconnected for longer than ~30s. Investigation in Phase 3A.7
            confirmed this is server-side and unrecoverable from the
            central regardless of GATT-level interventions tried.

            With 0 (the default), the bridge stays connected forever and
            relies on the remote's preferred slave-latency = 80 (configured
            after setup completes) to let the remote deep-sleep between
            conn events. Effective duty cycle is ~0.1%; CR2032 lifetime
            stays at ~10-15 months instead of ~18-24 months disconnected.
            Trade ~1.5x battery for 100% wake-press reliability.

            With a non-zero value (e.g. 60000), the bridge auto-disconnects
            after the idle window so the remote enters deep sleep. Battery
            is better, but any wake-press after >30s of disconnect drops
            the button identity (the synthesized `pickup` event still
            fires as a generic wake signal). Use this if battery matters
            more than knowing which button was pressed to wake the remote.

endmenu
